Healthy Home Visit (HHV) → Claims Conversion Dashboard

Business & Analytics Documentation

⸻

1. Business Context & Purpose

Why this dashboard exists

Healthy Home Visits (HHVs), conducted by vendors like Signify, are a key part of:
	•	Risk adjustment
	•	Care gap closure
	•	CMS reimbursement accuracy

However, HHVs only generate value if a corresponding medical claim is submitted and adjudicated.

This dashboard answers one core business question:

“Of the HHVs completed, how many actually resulted in claims — and how long did it take?”

⸻

2. Business Questions This Dashboard Answers
	1.	How many HHVs were completed?
	2.	How many of those HHVs converted into claims?
	3.	How many HHVs are still missing claims, and for how long?
	4.	Are claims being submitted within expected timelines (≤ 90 days)?
	5.	Where do delays cluster (by LOB, market, state, provider, CPT)?
	6.	Which HHVs need operational follow-up?

⸻

3. High-Level Data Logic (Non-Technical)

Core datasets used
	•	HHV disposition data → identifies completed visits
	•	Claims data → identifies claim submission and adjudication

How HHVs are matched to claims

An HHV is considered “converted” if:
	•	Member ID matches
	•	Date of Service matches
	•	Claim belongs to Signify (TIN-based filter)
	•	Claim contains core HHV CPT codes (e.g., 99342, 99344, 99348, 99336)
	•	Latest adjudicated claim is selected when multiple exist

⸻

4. Key Definitions (Business-Friendly)

Completed HHV

A Healthy Home Visit with a final “Completed” disposition.

HHV with Claim

A completed HHV where at least one valid claim was received for that visit.

Missing Claim

A completed HHV where:
	•	No matching claim has been received as of today
	•	OR claim has not arrived within the expected lag window

⚠️ Important:
“Missing” does not always mean “never coming” — it often reflects claim lag.

⸻

5. Claim Lag Concept (Critical to Understand)

Claims do not arrive instantly after an HHV.

Typical lifecycle:
	1.	HHV completed (Date of Service)
	2.	Vendor prepares documentation
	3.	Claim is submitted (can take weeks)
	4.	Claim adjudicated (paid / denied / adjusted)

Business expectation:
Most HHV claims should arrive within 90 days of Date of Service.

Why claims can look “missing” even if valid
	•	Claim still in submission window
	•	Claim submitted but not yet adjudicated
	•	Re-submission after denial
	•	Data latency in source systems

This dashboard explicitly separates timing effects from true gaps.

⸻

6. Dashboard Walk-Through (Visualization by Visualization)

⸻

6.1 KPI: Total Completed – Healthy Home Visits

What it shows
Total number of HHVs completed for the selected filters (LOB, market, plan, etc.)

Why it matters
This is the denominator for all downstream performance metrics.

Who uses it
	•	Leadership → program scale
	•	Operations → workload tracking

⸻

6.2 KPI: Total Completed – HHVs with Claims

What it shows
Number of completed HHVs that have at least one valid claim received.

Why it matters
This represents realized value, not just activity.

Key insight
	•	The gap between this and total HHVs highlights risk exposure

⸻

6.3 Trend: Claims Paid Over Time (by Date of Service)

What it shows
	•	Monthly trend of paid claims
	•	Split by MRA vs ACA Small Group

Why it matters
	•	Identifies seasonal patterns
	•	Highlights months where claims lag or drop
	•	Helps validate whether recent months are incomplete due to timing

Key insight
	•	Sharp drop in recent months ≠ failure
	•	Usually indicates expected runout

⸻

6.4 Map: Market Head (CMS State)

What it shows
	•	Geographic distribution of claims
	•	Based on CMS market / state alignment

Why it matters
	•	Identifies state-level performance gaps
	•	Supports market-specific escalation or vendor discussions

Leadership takeaway

“Which states consistently lag in HHV claim conversion?”

⸻

6.5 Table: Claims Status by LOB

What it shows
	•	Paid (P)
	•	Denied (D)
	•	Null / Pending

Why it matters
	•	Paid ≠ denied ≠ missing
	•	Helps distinguish process issues from eligibility issues

Operational insight
	•	High denial rate may indicate coding or documentation problems

⸻

6.6 Distribution: Claim Submission Lag Buckets

Example buckets
	•	0–30 days
	•	31–60 days
	•	61–90 days
	•	90+ days
	•	No claim received

Why it matters
	•	This is the most actionable visualization
	•	Separates normal lag from true risk

Key business rule
	•	HHVs > 90 days with no claim → requires follow-up

⸻

6.7 Trend: Members with HHV Completed but No Claims (90+ Days)

What it shows
	•	Monthly trend of HHVs aging past 90 days without claims

Why it matters
	•	Indicates vendor submission risk
	•	Drives compliance and escalation discussions

Leadership takeaway

“Are we accumulating financial exposure month over month?”

⸻

6.8 CPT-Level Detail (Supporting Table)

What it shows
	•	Core HHV CPT codes linked to claims

Why it matters
	•	Confirms correct identification of HHV claims
	•	Helps exclude screening-only claims

