1️⃣ Annual HHV Eligibility File (Aetna → Signify)
	•	Aetna generates and shares an annual eligibility list of members who are eligible for a Healthy Home Visit.
	•	This list is shared in bulk at the beginning of the program year with Signify.
	•	The list represents:
	•	Who is eligible
	•	Who should be targeted for HHVs
	•	This list does NOT indicate that a visit occurred — only that a member is eligible.

📌 Key point:
This file defines the universe of potential HHVs, not completed visits.

⸻

2️⃣ HHV Disposition Data (Signify → Aetna)
	•	Signify determines how and when visits are conducted based on operational workflows.
	•	Once visits occur, Signify sends monthly disposition files back to Aetna.
	•	Disposition data includes:
	•	Member identifier
	•	Visit status (Completed, Cancelled, In Progress, etc.)
	•	Date of Service (HHV visit date)
	•	Program / Line of Business attributes

📌 Disposition data is the authoritative source for:
	•	Which HHVs actually occurred
	•	When the visit happened
	•	Which HHVs are considered “Completed”

➡️ This dashboard uses ONLY “Completed” dispositions to identify valid HHVs.

⸻

3️⃣ Claims Data (Signify → Aetna Claims Systems)
	•	After an HHV is completed:
	•	The Signify-affiliated provider (e.g., nurse practitioner) submits a medical claim.
	•	Claims are:
	•	Submitted separately from disposition data
	•	Processed through standard Aetna claims adjudication systems
	•	Claims data includes:
	•	Member ID
	•	Date of Service
	•	CPT / Procedure codes
	•	Provider identifiers (TIN/NPI)
	•	Claim status (Paid, Denied, Adjusted, etc.)

📌 Important distinction:
	•	Disposition data confirms a visit happened
	•	Claims data confirms the visit was billed and adjudicated



